Call sugar: `x.f` is `{f x}`, and at the head of a brace `{x.f a b}` is `{f x a b}`. `f` is found by ordinary
lexical lookup (never by the type of x), so the sugar works for every type and changes no semantics.
$[Import* std]
${"Hello!".substr 0 3} ${"Hello!".chars.length} ${"a,b,,c".split ","}
expect: "Hel"
expect: 6
expect: ["a", "b", "", "c"]
Chains: every step but the last takes only the receiver; braces can be receivers too.
${{' 1 2 3}.map {^ {x} {* x x}}} ${{{' 1 2 3 4}.filter {^ {x} {> x 2}}}.length} ${3.neg} ${2.5.floor}
expect: [1, 4, 9]
expect: 2
expect: -3
expect: 2
Outside the head, `x.f` is a one-argument call: here `xs.reverse` is the receiver-only call `{reverse xs}`.
$[# xs {' 1 2 3}]
${append xs xs.reverse} ${xs.std::length}
expect: [1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1]
expect: 3
Whitespace matters: `{r .x}` applies r to the getter `.x`; `{.x r}` reads the field.
$[# {twice f} {^ {r} {* 2 {f r}}}]
${{twice .x} {. x 21}} ${.x {. x 1}}
expect: 42
expect: 1
Objects are records; methods are fields holding functions. Instances made from a prototype share one closure:
`.name=` copies only the record's field table (values are shared, never deep-copied).
$[# {_greet self} [f "Hi, I am ${.name self}."]]
$[# Person {. name "nobody" greet _greet}]
$[# ada {.name= Person "Ada"}]
${{.greet ada} ada} ${{.greet Person} Person}
expect: "Hi, I am Ada."
expect: "Hi, I am nobody."
Macros see the sugar as ordinary calls, so it works inside templates (and is hygienic there).
$[Macro !firstThree {{s} {s.substr 0 3}}]
${!firstThree "Lamlet"}
expect: "Lam"
